ML_PAGE("l9-subjective", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l9\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">CHAPTER 9 · SUBJECTIVE EXAM PREPARATION</span><h2>Writing Answers That Earn Marks</h2><p>Define, explain the mechanism, apply it to the scenario, then evaluate and conclude. Each mock question below has its full model answer behind a reveal; the complete subjective notes are underneath.</p></header><div class=\"theme-l9\"><div class=\"card accent-card mb-20\">\n    <h3>The four-move answer (10-20 mark questions)</h3>\n    <div class=\"flow-grid\">\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>1. Establish the principle</b><p>Define the concept in the chapter's terms: the information set of each form of efficiency, abnormal (risk-adjusted, after-cost) return, the named bias, or the indicator's exact formula.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>2. Explain the mechanism</b><p>Show <em>why</em>: competition incorporates information into prices; a bias tilts judgment the same way for many investors; an indicator compares issues, volume or cash for a stated reason.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>3. Apply and analyse</b><p>Connect it to the scenario: what information was available and when, the calculation ($A - D$, TRIN, MFCR, SMA, OBV, excess return), and the specific behaviour that reveals the bias.</p></div>\n        <div class=\"flow-card\"><b>4. Evaluate and conclude</b><p>Weigh alternative explanations (missing risk, chance, costs, sample), state what the evidence does <em>not</em> show, and give a conditional conclusion or a testable plan.</p></div>\n    </div>\n    <p class=\"card-note\">Strong Chapter 9 answers keep five things apart: <b>information available → price response → risk-adjusted return → costs → evidence</b>. Most weak answers jump from \"the price rose\" straight to \"the market is inefficient\" or from \"the indicator is bullish\" straight to \"the price will rise\".</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"compare-box span-all\">\n    <div class=\"compare-side\">\n        <h4>Weak answer</h4>\n        <p><b>Q:</b> Orion publishes unexpectedly strong earnings and its share price rises immediately. A trader says buying after publication must earn an abnormal return because good earnings make prices rise. Evaluate.</p>\n        <p>\"The share price rose after the announcement, so the market is inefficient and the trader is right that buying good-earnings stocks makes money.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-weak\">Confuses a price reaction with an exploitable opportunity; no information set, no benchmark, no costs.</p>\n    </div>\n    <div class=\"compare-side highlight\">\n        <h4>Strong answer</h4>\n        <p>\"Semi-strong efficiency says public information is incorporated quickly, so the relevant test is how fast and how accurately the price absorbs the <b>unanticipated</b> part of the announcement. An immediate rise is exactly that adjustment and is consistent with semi-strong efficiency. It does not follow that buying <b>after</b> publication earns an abnormal return: the new price already reflects the news. Only a persistent, repeatable after-cost return above a suitable risk benchmark, found by a pre-specified rule and confirmed on new samples, would challenge efficiency, and even then it could reflect an omitted risk or selection bias. The trader's claim is therefore unsupported.\"</p>\n        <p class=\"note-good\">Information set + mechanism + what the evidence does not show + qualified conclusion.</p>\n    </div>\n</div>\n</div><h3 class=\"block-title\">Mock examination: questions with model answers</h3><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><span>Information and market efficiency (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Orion publishes unexpectedly strong earnings at 9:00 a.m. Its share price rises immediately. A trader argues that buying after publication must produce an abnormal return because \"good earnings make prices rise.\" A second investor says that a company executive could use non-public earnings information before release. Assume both assertions concern repeated risk-adjusted performance, not a single lucky trade.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Distinguish weak, semi-strong and strong forms of market efficiency, naming the information set for each.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Explain whether an immediate response to the surprise is compatible with semi-strong efficiency and why the trader's conclusion does not necessarily follow.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Explain how the executive's hypothetical use of non-public information relates to the strong form and why one profitable trade is not a sufficient test.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate the claim that market efficiency means prices never change and fundamental analysis has no role.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Information sets [6].</strong> Weak-form efficiency means past market trading data, including past prices and volume, are reflected in prices; trading using that information alone should not consistently earn abnormal risk-adjusted returns after relevant costs. Semi-strong efficiency extends this to all public information, such as financial releases, in addition to past trading data. Strong-form efficiency extends it to public <strong>and private</strong> information. These information sets are nested: a stronger form includes the information set of the weaker form. <em>Marking: 2 marks per correctly distinguished form.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Earnings release [5].</strong> An unexpected earnings announcement changes investors' expectations about future cash flows and risk, so an immediate price adjustment is consistent with semi-strong efficiency. Once public information is rapidly incorporated, the fact that a positive surprise moved the price does not imply buying afterward will reliably earn abnormal returns. The outcome depends on the new price, information not yet incorporated, subsequent events and costs. <em>Marking: compatible adjustment 2, surprise versus later opportunity 2, uncertainty or costs 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Private information [4].</strong> Persistent abnormal risk-adjusted gains using genuinely private information would contradict the strong form under the test's assumptions. One profit could result from chance or risk exposure and is insufficient evidence of repeatable outperformance. Misuse of material non-public information can also be unlawful depending on applicable rules; this hypothetical is not a recommended strategy. <em>Marking: strong-form connection 2, single-observation limitation 1, private/public distinction 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluate [5].</strong> Efficiency does not mean a fixed price: new, unexpected information can rationally change expected cash flows or discount rates and therefore price. Fundamental analysis can contribute to price discovery and help an investor understand risk and suitability, although competing analysts and costs can make repeatable abnormal gains hard to obtain. Efficiency is a hypothesis about information incorporation, not a guarantee that a particular price is exactly correct. <em>Marking: changing prices 2, role of analysis 2, qualification 1.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><span>An anomaly and its practical value (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A researcher backtests a strategy that buys shares after unexpectedly good earnings. In the sample, its average return for a specified holding period is 3.2% per trade; a risk benchmark estimates 2.4% for comparable exposure, and estimated total trading costs for that holding period are 0.5% of capital. Assume simple arithmetic differences for this exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [4] Calculate the gross and after-cost excess return relative to the stated benchmark.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Explain how a persistent price drift after an earnings announcement might challenge semi-strong efficiency, and what would need to be observed in practice.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [6] Discuss risk-model error, chance or repeated testing, and transaction costs as alternative reasons the apparent opportunity may not be exploitable.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a credible follow-up test, specifying at least three features and what an adverse result would imply.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Excess returns [4].</strong> Gross excess return is $3.2\\%-2.4\\%=0.8$ percentage points. After the stated 0.5% trading cost, net strategy return is $2.7\\%$, and net excess return is $2.7\\%-2.4\\%=\\mathbf{0.3}$ percentage points per stated holding period. This assumes the benchmark is comparable and that the cost estimate applies to each trade over the whole holding period. <em>Marking: gross excess 1, net strategy 1, net excess 1, interpretation/assumption 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Drift [5].</strong> Post-earnings announcement drift describes price movement continuing in the direction of an earnings surprise after the public announcement. If a feasible strategy established after information becomes public repeatedly earns positive abnormal returns net of trading costs, it challenges a simple semi-strong-efficiency interpretation. It is necessary to establish the event time, realistic execution price, holding horizon, risk adjustment and reliability across samples. <em>Marking: concept 2, test implication 2, implementability 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Competing explanations [6].</strong> The benchmark may fail to capture systematic risks or other exposures, so measured \"abnormal\" return may be risk compensation. Searching many patterns can produce apparently successful results by chance; a selected historical winner may fail on new data. Trading costs, spreads, slippage, delays, limited liquidity and market impact may consume the gross return. A 0.3-point estimated net return could be particularly sensitive to measurement error. <em>Marking: 2 each for risk, chance/data selection, and implementation costs.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Research design [5].</strong> Define the trading rule, event time, risk adjustment and costs in advance; test on an independent later sample and multiple settings; use realistic executable prices, liquidity constraints and full costs; assess statistical uncertainty rather than only the average. Failure to reproduce a reliable positive after-cost excess return weakens the claim of an exploitable opportunity, although it does not prove perfect market efficiency. <em>Marking: three test features 3, interpretation 1, qualified inference 1.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><span>Behavioral traps and controls (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>Maya credits her skill for three profitable trades but blames market manipulation for two losses. She buys more of her employer's shares because \"I know the company,\" holds a losing stock solely to avoid realizing a loss, and assumes a new company's product will succeed because its first two customer reviews are positive. She ignores a credible report contradicting her initial view.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Identify and explain three distinct biases shown in the scenario, linking each to the specific behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Identify two further behavioral tendencies that may be relevant and explain them.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain how one or more of these tendencies could affect portfolio decisions or trading costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a decision process with at least three concrete controls. Explain how each responds to a diagnosed problem.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Three biases [6].</strong> Maya's self-attribution bias credits success to skill while assigning losses to external factors. Familiarity bias leads her to prefer employer stock simply because it is known, despite concentration and valuation risks. Loss aversion may contribute to holding solely to avoid realizing a loss: the past purchase price dominates consideration of future prospects. These diagnoses are plausible inferences from the described behavior, not clinical conclusions. <em>Marking: 2 marks for each correctly named and applied bias.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Further tendencies [4].</strong> Representativeness appears when Maya extrapolates broadly from only two positive reviews, ignoring sample size and chance. Belief perseverance or confirmation bias appears when she disregards credible contrary evidence because it conflicts with her view. <em>Marking: 2 marks per tendency and scenario link.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Consequences [5].</strong> Concentration in an employer's shares links both employment income and portfolio value to one business. Refusing to reassess a losing share can leave capital exposed to a weak future opportunity; selling solely because of a loss would also be unjustified. Overconfidence and selective attribution can encourage excess trading and recurring spreads, commissions and taxes where applicable. <em>Marking: portfolio consequence 2, trading-cost mechanism 2, balanced qualification 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Controls [5].</strong> Set a position-size limit and assess employer exposure across salary and investments; write the investment thesis, forecast and conditions for revisiting it before a trade; actively seek and record contrary evidence; review decisions using a benchmark and include costs; use a scheduled review rather than trade in response to every price move. Each control addresses concentration, hindsight, confirmation bias or excessive trading. <em>Marking: three concrete linked controls 3, appropriate reasoning 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><span>Breadth, volume and indicators (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>On a trading day 1,200 stocks advance and 800 decline. Advancing stocks trade 360 million shares and declining stocks trade 120 million. A mutual-fund group holds RM80 million in cash and has RM800 million in total assets. For this exercise define:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$TRIN=\\frac{\\text{advancing issues}/\\text{declining issues}}{\\text{advancing volume}/\\text{declining volume}},\\qquad MFCR=\\frac{\\text{fund cash}}{\\text{fund total assets}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate net advances, TRIN and MFCR.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Explain what the two components of TRIN compare and interpret the result relative to 1 using the chapter's convention.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Explain the chapter's possible bullish interpretation of a high cash ratio and give one alternative explanation.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Explain why a positive breadth reading and low TRIN cannot establish that tomorrow's market return will be positive. State two further checks.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Values [6].</strong> Net advances $=1,200-800=\\mathbf{400}$ issues. The issue ratio is $1,200/800=1.5$ and volume ratio is $360/120=3.0$, so $TRIN=1.5/3.0=\\mathbf{0.50}$. The cash ratio is $MFCR=80/800=\\mathbf{10\\%}$. <em>Marking: 2 marks per result and working.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Meaning [5].</strong> The first ratio compares how many issues rose with how many fell; the second compares the volume traded in those groups. At 0.50, advancing volume is high relative to advancing issue breadth under this calculation. The chapter associates lower TRIN values with bullish conditions and higher values with bearish conditions, but a one-day reading is an indicator, not a reliable promise of future returns. <em>Marking: two components 2, numerical interpretation 2, limitation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Cash ratio [4].</strong> In the chapter's interpretation, a larger cash position might provide purchasing power for future purchases. But managers may hold cash to meet withdrawals, manage risk, or await opportunities, and neither the timing nor direction of deployment is known. A single level must be interpreted relative to comparable historical data and fund mandates. <em>Marking: potential bullish mechanism 2, credible alternative and limitation 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Limits [5].</strong> The indicators summarize a single day's breadth and volume, not tomorrow's future information or return. They can change, conflict with other indicators, or lack predictive power after costs and risk. Check the history and out-of-sample performance of the specific rule, and examine the trend, market context, data quality and realistic trading costs. <em>Marking: no guaranteed prediction 2, two checks 2, costs/uncertainty 1.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><span>Price patterns and trend signals (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A stock closes at RM10, RM11, RM10, RM12 and RM13 on Days 1-5 respectively. Volume on Days 2-5 is 1 million, 2 million, 3 million and 4 million shares. For this exercise set on-balance volume (OBV) to zero at Day 1; add the day's volume for a higher close and subtract it for a lower close. A trader identifies a prior resistance area near RM12 and observes a Day 5 close at RM13.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the five-day and three-day simple moving averages on Day 5.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate OBV after each of Days 2-5 and interpret its direction over those days.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain resistance, a possible breakout and the trader's interpretation of this price sequence. State what the evidence does not show.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a test of whether the rule provides value after costs and risk. Explain how it differs from fundamental analysis.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Moving averages [5].</strong> The Day 5 five-day simple average is $(10+11+10+12+13)/5=\\mathbf{RM11.20}$. The Day 5 three-day average uses Days 3-5: $(10+12+13)/3=\\mathbf{RM11.67}$. Each is an average of past closes, not a prediction by itself. <em>Marking: 2 each for calculation and 1 for timing/interpretation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) OBV [5].</strong> Start at zero on Day 1. Day 2 rises: $0+1=1$ million. Day 3 falls: $1-2=-1$ million. Day 4 rises: $-1+3=2$ million. Day 5 rises: $2+4=\\mathbf{6}$ million. The running measure rises overall from the starting value, but its absolute level depends on the arbitrary starting point; its movement is the focus. <em>Marking: 1 per day and 1 for direction/qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Resistance [5].</strong> A resistance area is a price region where prior selling pressure has historically limited advances. A close at RM13 above the stated RM12 area could be interpreted as a possible upward breakout, and both moving averages are below the last close. The five observations do not establish that the breakout will persist, that resistance is objectively fixed, or that a profitable after-cost rule exists. <em>Marking: concept 2, scenario interpretation 1, two limitations 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Research design [5].</strong> Specify the entry, exit, position size, signal timing and price available for execution before testing. Test on independent data, include spread, commissions, slippage, benchmark and risk adjustment, and report uncertainty and failed periods. Fundamental analysis instead focuses on expected cash flows, financial condition, competitive position and valuation; a chart rule focuses on market-price and volume patterns. The approaches may be combined, but their predictions are not guaranteed. <em>Marking: pre-specified rule 1, independent test 1, costs and risk 1, fundamental comparison 2.</em></p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><span>Integrated investment process (20 marks)</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body markdown-body compact\"><p>A colleague wants to invest all savings in a familiar technology stock. It has risen for six months, a chart appears to break resistance, and a forum claims \"everyone knows the next product will succeed.\" The colleague cites a large short interest as guaranteed proof that the price must rise. The investor needs some of the money within one year.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Identify two behavioral traps and explain how each might affect the decision.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Analyse two different interpretations of short interest. Correctly explain the transaction required to close a short position.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Assess what the chart and publicly available product claim do and do not establish about expected abnormal returns.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a balanced action plan considering evidence, concentration, time horizon and decision rules; explain one condition under which the colleague should reduce exposure.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<hr /><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal the model answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>(a) Biases [5].</strong> Familiarity bias may cause the colleague to infer quality or fair value merely from knowing the stock. Herding or representativeness may lead them to extrapolate six months of price rises or follow a forum's consensus without sufficient evidence. A momentum observation can be data, but treating it as certainty is the error. <em>Marking: 2 marks per identified and applied trap, 1 for qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Short interest [5].</strong> A large short position can represent pessimism: short sellers expect a price decline. It can also represent potential future buying demand because a short position is ordinarily closed by <strong>buying</strong> shares to return borrowed shares; short covering is buying, not future sales. Neither interpretation guarantees a rise: shorts may remain open, new selling may occur, and prices respond to many forces. <em>Marking: pessimism 1, future demand 1, correct covering transaction 2, uncertainty 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evidence [5].</strong> A chart breakout describes past price behavior; to claim an advantage, test a defined rule net of risk and costs. A public claim about a product might already be reflected in price, and popularity does not establish reliable earnings or fair valuation. Obtain credible evidence on the product, financial prospects and current price, distinguish what is new from what is expected, and assess competing scenarios. <em>Marking: chart limitation 2, public information and price 2, evidence 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Action plan [5].</strong> Separate the money required within one year and evaluate its liquidity and loss tolerance before taking stock risk. Assess the business and valuation, compare opportunities, set a diversified allocation and position-size limit, and write conditions for reviewing the thesis. Reduce exposure if a near-term cash need would force a sale after a decline, or if the position exceeds the investor's capacity for loss. Apply a consistent plan rather than allowing a forum or single indicator to decide. <em>Marking: horizon and liquidity 1, evidence and valuation 1, diversification/size 1, decision rules 1, justified reduction 1.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l9\" id=\"l9s-toc\"><summary><strong>Notes cover</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">6 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Explaining a market claim instead of repeating it\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l9s-s1\" title=\"Explaining a market claim instead of repeating it\">Explain a market claim, don't repeat it</button></li><li data-full=\"Mock subjective examination\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l9s-s2\" title=\"Mock subjective examination\">Mock examination: six questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Model answers and illustrative marking guidance\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l9s-s3\" title=\"Model answers and illustrative marking guidance\">Model answers and marking guidance</button></li><li data-full=\"Short-answer revision\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l9s-s4\" title=\"Short-answer revision\">Short-answer revision</button></li><li data-full=\"Indicator and evidence guide\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l9s-s5\" title=\"Indicator and evidence guide\">Indicator and evidence guide</button></li><li data-full=\"Final integrated exercise\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l9s-s6\" title=\"Final integrated exercise\">Final integrated exercise</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"full-notes theme-l9\" open><summary><div><strong>Complete Chapter 9 subjective notes</strong><small>Every part of the subjective preparation, one dropdown per part.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>6 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><h2>Chapter 9 - Subjective Exam Preparation</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l9s-s1\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Explaining a market claim instead of repeating it</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This chapter asks whether investors can reliably turn information or patterns into superior returns, and how their own judgment can interfere. A strong answer distinguishes <strong>information available, price response, risk-adjusted return, costs and evidence</strong>.</p>\n<p>A weak answer says, \"The share price rose after the announcement, so the market is inefficient.\" A stronger answer says, \"The relevant test is how quickly and accurately the price incorporates the unanticipated public announcement. A rise when good news appears is consistent with adjustment; a persistent, repeatable after-cost abnormal return after the release would need further investigation, including the risk benchmark and possible selection bias.\"</p>\n<p>The six original scenarios and their marks are illustrative practice; no official paper or marking scheme is assumed. Attempt the questions before reading the separate model answers. Unless specified, transaction-cost estimates are applied to the <strong>whole stated return period</strong> and numerical ratios are rounded to two decimal places.</p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l9s-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Mock subjective examination</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Question 1 - Information and market efficiency (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Orion publishes unexpectedly strong earnings at 9:00 a.m. Its share price rises immediately. A trader argues that buying after publication must produce an abnormal return because \"good earnings make prices rise.\" A second investor says that a company executive could use non-public earnings information before release. Assume both assertions concern repeated risk-adjusted performance, not a single lucky trade.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Distinguish weak, semi-strong and strong forms of market efficiency, naming the information set for each.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Explain whether an immediate response to the surprise is compatible with semi-strong efficiency and why the trader's conclusion does not necessarily follow.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Explain how the executive's hypothetical use of non-public information relates to the strong form and why one profitable trade is not a sufficient test.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Evaluate the claim that market efficiency means prices never change and fundamental analysis has no role.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Understand → Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 2 - An anomaly and its practical value (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A researcher backtests a strategy that buys shares after unexpectedly good earnings. In the sample, its average return for a specified holding period is 3.2% per trade; a risk benchmark estimates 2.4% for comparable exposure, and estimated total trading costs for that holding period are 0.5% of capital. Assume simple arithmetic differences for this exercise.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [4] Calculate the gross and after-cost excess return relative to the stated benchmark.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Explain how a persistent price drift after an earnings announcement might challenge semi-strong efficiency, and what would need to be observed in practice.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [6] Discuss risk-model error, chance or repeated testing, and transaction costs as alternative reasons the apparent opportunity may not be exploitable.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a credible follow-up test, specifying at least three features and what an adverse result would imply.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 3 - Behavioral traps and controls (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>Maya credits her skill for three profitable trades but blames market manipulation for two losses. She buys more of her employer's shares because \"I know the company,\" holds a losing stock solely to avoid realizing a loss, and assumes a new company's product will succeed because its first two customer reviews are positive. She ignores a credible report contradicting her initial view.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Identify and explain three distinct biases shown in the scenario, linking each to the specific behavior.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [4] Identify two further behavioral tendencies that may be relevant and explain them.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain how one or more of these tendencies could affect portfolio decisions or trading costs.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a decision process with at least three concrete controls. Explain how each responds to a diagnosed problem.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 4 - Breadth, volume and indicators (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>On a trading day 1,200 stocks advance and 800 decline. Advancing stocks trade 360 million shares and declining stocks trade 120 million. A mutual-fund group holds RM80 million in cash and has RM800 million in total assets. For this exercise define:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$TRIN=\\frac{\\text{advancing issues}/\\text{declining issues}}{\\text{advancing volume}/\\text{declining volume}},\\qquad MFCR=\\frac{\\text{fund cash}}{\\text{fund total assets}}$$</div>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [6] Calculate net advances, TRIN and MFCR.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Explain what the two components of TRIN compare and interpret the result relative to 1 using the chapter's convention.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [4] Explain the chapter's possible bullish interpretation of a high cash ratio and give one alternative explanation.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Explain why a positive breadth reading and low TRIN cannot establish that tomorrow's market return will be positive. State two further checks.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 5 - Price patterns and trend signals (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A stock closes at RM10, RM11, RM10, RM12 and RM13 on Days 1-5 respectively. Volume on Days 2-5 is 1 million, 2 million, 3 million and 4 million shares. For this exercise set on-balance volume (OBV) to zero at Day 1; add the day's volume for a higher close and subtract it for a lower close. A trader identifies a prior resistance area near RM12 and observes a Day 5 close at RM13.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Calculate the five-day and three-day simple moving averages on Day 5.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Calculate OBV after each of Days 2-5 and interpret its direction over those days.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Explain resistance, a possible breakout and the trader's interpretation of this price sequence. State what the evidence does not show.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a test of whether the rule provides value after costs and risk. Explain how it differs from fundamental analysis.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Apply → Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<h4>Question 6 - Integrated investment process (20 marks)</h4>\n<p>A colleague wants to invest all savings in a familiar technology stock. It has risen for six months, a chart appears to break resistance, and a forum claims \"everyone knows the next product will succeed.\" The colleague cites a large short interest as guaranteed proof that the price must rise. The investor needs some of the money within one year.</p>\n<p><strong>(a)</strong> [5] Identify two behavioral traps and explain how each might affect the decision.</p>\n<p><strong>(b)</strong> [5] Analyse two different interpretations of short interest. Correctly explain the transaction required to close a short position.</p>\n<p><strong>(c)</strong> [5] Assess what the chart and publicly available product claim do and do not establish about expected abnormal returns.</p>\n<p><strong>(d)</strong> [5] Design a balanced action plan considering evidence, concentration, time horizon and decision rules; explain one condition under which the colleague should reduce exposure.</p>\n<p><em>Bloom focus: Analyse → Evaluate → Create.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l9s-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Model answers and illustrative marking guidance</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Answer 1 - Efficiency and information</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Information sets [6].</strong> Weak-form efficiency means past market trading data, including past prices and volume, are reflected in prices; trading using that information alone should not consistently earn abnormal risk-adjusted returns after relevant costs. Semi-strong efficiency extends this to all public information, such as financial releases, in addition to past trading data. Strong-form efficiency extends it to public <strong>and private</strong> information. These information sets are nested: a stronger form includes the information set of the weaker form. <em>Marking: 2 marks per correctly distinguished form.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Earnings release [5].</strong> An unexpected earnings announcement changes investors' expectations about future cash flows and risk, so an immediate price adjustment is consistent with semi-strong efficiency. Once public information is rapidly incorporated, the fact that a positive surprise moved the price does not imply buying afterward will reliably earn abnormal returns. The outcome depends on the new price, information not yet incorporated, subsequent events and costs. <em>Marking: compatible adjustment 2, surprise versus later opportunity 2, uncertainty or costs 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Private information [4].</strong> Persistent abnormal risk-adjusted gains using genuinely private information would contradict the strong form under the test's assumptions. One profit could result from chance or risk exposure and is insufficient evidence of repeatable outperformance. Misuse of material non-public information can also be unlawful depending on applicable rules; this hypothetical is not a recommended strategy. <em>Marking: strong-form connection 2, single-observation limitation 1, private/public distinction 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Evaluate [5].</strong> Efficiency does not mean a fixed price: new, unexpected information can rationally change expected cash flows or discount rates and therefore price. Fundamental analysis can contribute to price discovery and help an investor understand risk and suitability, although competing analysts and costs can make repeatable abnormal gains hard to obtain. Efficiency is a hypothesis about information incorporation, not a guarantee that a particular price is exactly correct. <em>Marking: changing prices 2, role of analysis 2, qualification 1.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 2 - Evidence versus an exploitable anomaly</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Excess returns [4].</strong> Gross excess return is $3.2\\%-2.4\\%=0.8$ percentage points. After the stated 0.5% trading cost, net strategy return is $2.7\\%$, and net excess return is $2.7\\%-2.4\\%=\\mathbf{0.3}$ percentage points per stated holding period. This assumes the benchmark is comparable and that the cost estimate applies to each trade over the whole holding period. <em>Marking: gross excess 1, net strategy 1, net excess 1, interpretation/assumption 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Drift [5].</strong> Post-earnings announcement drift describes price movement continuing in the direction of an earnings surprise after the public announcement. If a feasible strategy established after information becomes public repeatedly earns positive abnormal returns net of trading costs, it challenges a simple semi-strong-efficiency interpretation. It is necessary to establish the event time, realistic execution price, holding horizon, risk adjustment and reliability across samples. <em>Marking: concept 2, test implication 2, implementability 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Competing explanations [6].</strong> The benchmark may fail to capture systematic risks or other exposures, so measured \"abnormal\" return may be risk compensation. Searching many patterns can produce apparently successful results by chance; a selected historical winner may fail on new data. Trading costs, spreads, slippage, delays, limited liquidity and market impact may consume the gross return. A 0.3-point estimated net return could be particularly sensitive to measurement error. <em>Marking: 2 each for risk, chance/data selection, and implementation costs.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Research design [5].</strong> Define the trading rule, event time, risk adjustment and costs in advance; test on an independent later sample and multiple settings; use realistic executable prices, liquidity constraints and full costs; assess statistical uncertainty rather than only the average. Failure to reproduce a reliable positive after-cost excess return weakens the claim of an exploitable opportunity, although it does not prove perfect market efficiency. <em>Marking: three test features 3, interpretation 1, qualified inference 1.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 3 - Bias and debiasing</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Three biases [6].</strong> Maya's self-attribution bias credits success to skill while assigning losses to external factors. Familiarity bias leads her to prefer employer stock simply because it is known, despite concentration and valuation risks. Loss aversion may contribute to holding solely to avoid realizing a loss: the past purchase price dominates consideration of future prospects. These diagnoses are plausible inferences from the described behavior, not clinical conclusions. <em>Marking: 2 marks for each correctly named and applied bias.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Further tendencies [4].</strong> Representativeness appears when Maya extrapolates broadly from only two positive reviews, ignoring sample size and chance. Belief perseverance or confirmation bias appears when she disregards credible contrary evidence because it conflicts with her view. <em>Marking: 2 marks per tendency and scenario link.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Consequences [5].</strong> Concentration in an employer's shares links both employment income and portfolio value to one business. Refusing to reassess a losing share can leave capital exposed to a weak future opportunity; selling solely because of a loss would also be unjustified. Overconfidence and selective attribution can encourage excess trading and recurring spreads, commissions and taxes where applicable. <em>Marking: portfolio consequence 2, trading-cost mechanism 2, balanced qualification 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Controls [5].</strong> Set a position-size limit and assess employer exposure across salary and investments; write the investment thesis, forecast and conditions for revisiting it before a trade; actively seek and record contrary evidence; review decisions using a benchmark and include costs; use a scheduled review rather than trade in response to every price move. Each control addresses concentration, hindsight, confirmation bias or excessive trading. <em>Marking: three concrete linked controls 3, appropriate reasoning 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 4 - Breadth and indicator calculations</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Values [6].</strong> Net advances $=1,200-800=\\mathbf{400}$ issues. The issue ratio is $1,200/800=1.5$ and volume ratio is $360/120=3.0$, so $TRIN=1.5/3.0=\\mathbf{0.50}$. The cash ratio is $MFCR=80/800=\\mathbf{10\\%}$. <em>Marking: 2 marks per result and working.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Meaning [5].</strong> The first ratio compares how many issues rose with how many fell; the second compares the volume traded in those groups. At 0.50, advancing volume is high relative to advancing issue breadth under this calculation. The chapter associates lower TRIN values with bullish conditions and higher values with bearish conditions, but a one-day reading is an indicator, not a reliable promise of future returns. <em>Marking: two components 2, numerical interpretation 2, limitation 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Cash ratio [4].</strong> In the chapter's interpretation, a larger cash position might provide purchasing power for future purchases. But managers may hold cash to meet withdrawals, manage risk, or await opportunities, and neither the timing nor direction of deployment is known. A single level must be interpreted relative to comparable historical data and fund mandates. <em>Marking: potential bullish mechanism 2, credible alternative and limitation 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Limits [5].</strong> The indicators summarize a single day's breadth and volume, not tomorrow's future information or return. They can change, conflict with other indicators, or lack predictive power after costs and risk. Check the history and out-of-sample performance of the specific rule, and examine the trend, market context, data quality and realistic trading costs. <em>Marking: no guaranteed prediction 2, two checks 2, costs/uncertainty 1.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 5 - Moving averages, OBV and chart interpretation</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Moving averages [5].</strong> The Day 5 five-day simple average is $(10+11+10+12+13)/5=\\mathbf{RM11.20}$. The Day 5 three-day average uses Days 3-5: $(10+12+13)/3=\\mathbf{RM11.67}$. Each is an average of past closes, not a prediction by itself. <em>Marking: 2 each for calculation and 1 for timing/interpretation.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) OBV [5].</strong> Start at zero on Day 1. Day 2 rises: $0+1=1$ million. Day 3 falls: $1-2=-1$ million. Day 4 rises: $-1+3=2$ million. Day 5 rises: $2+4=\\mathbf{6}$ million. The running measure rises overall from the starting value, but its absolute level depends on the arbitrary starting point; its movement is the focus. <em>Marking: 1 per day and 1 for direction/qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Resistance [5].</strong> A resistance area is a price region where prior selling pressure has historically limited advances. A close at RM13 above the stated RM12 area could be interpreted as a possible upward breakout, and both moving averages are below the last close. The five observations do not establish that the breakout will persist, that resistance is objectively fixed, or that a profitable after-cost rule exists. <em>Marking: concept 2, scenario interpretation 1, two limitations 2.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Research design [5].</strong> Specify the entry, exit, position size, signal timing and price available for execution before testing. Test on independent data, include spread, commissions, slippage, benchmark and risk adjustment, and report uncertainty and failed periods. Fundamental analysis instead focuses on expected cash flows, financial condition, competitive position and valuation; a chart rule focuses on market-price and volume patterns. The approaches may be combined, but their predictions are not guaranteed. <em>Marking: pre-specified rule 1, independent test 1, costs and risk 1, fundamental comparison 2.</em></p>\n<h4>Answer 6 - An integrated investment decision</h4>\n<p><strong>(a) Biases [5].</strong> Familiarity bias may cause the colleague to infer quality or fair value merely from knowing the stock. Herding or representativeness may lead them to extrapolate six months of price rises or follow a forum's consensus without sufficient evidence. A momentum observation can be data, but treating it as certainty is the error. <em>Marking: 2 marks per identified and applied trap, 1 for qualification.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(b) Short interest [5].</strong> A large short position can represent pessimism: short sellers expect a price decline. It can also represent potential future buying demand because a short position is ordinarily closed by <strong>buying</strong> shares to return borrowed shares; short covering is buying, not future sales. Neither interpretation guarantees a rise: shorts may remain open, new selling may occur, and prices respond to many forces. <em>Marking: pessimism 1, future demand 1, correct covering transaction 2, uncertainty 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(c) Evidence [5].</strong> A chart breakout describes past price behavior; to claim an advantage, test a defined rule net of risk and costs. A public claim about a product might already be reflected in price, and popularity does not establish reliable earnings or fair valuation. Obtain credible evidence on the product, financial prospects and current price, distinguish what is new from what is expected, and assess competing scenarios. <em>Marking: chart limitation 2, public information and price 2, evidence 1.</em></p>\n<p><strong>(d) Action plan [5].</strong> Separate the money required within one year and evaluate its liquidity and loss tolerance before taking stock risk. Assess the business and valuation, compare opportunities, set a diversified allocation and position-size limit, and write conditions for reviewing the thesis. Reduce exposure if a near-term cash need would force a sale after a decline, or if the position exceeds the investor's capacity for loss. Apply a consistent plan rather than allowing a forum or single indicator to decide. <em>Marking: horizon and liquidity 1, evidence and valuation 1, diversification/size 1, decision rules 1, justified reduction 1.</em></p>\n<hr /></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l9s-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Short-answer revision</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><ol>\n<li>What information distinguishes weak, semi-strong and strong forms of efficiency?</li>\n<li>Why can an efficient-market price change when surprising news arrives?</li>\n<li>How can a pattern be statistically interesting yet uneconomic to trade?</li>\n<li>Distinguish loss aversion, overconfidence and familiarity bias.</li>\n<li>Why may short interest be interpreted as both pessimism and potential future demand?</li>\n<li>What does an advance-decline measure count that volume does not?</li>\n<li>What does a moving average smooth, and what does it not prove?</li>\n<li>Why is a backtest on new data useful when investigating an anomaly?</li>\n</ol></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l9s-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Indicator and evidence guide</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l9_breadth\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Breadth calculator: A-D line and TRIN</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Edit five days of advances, declines and volume; the A-D line, net breadth and TRIN update live.</figcaption></figure></div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Task</th>\n<th>Method used in these questions</th>\n<th>Interpretation limit</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Daily breadth</td>\n<td>Advancing issues - declining issues</td>\n<td>A positive number summarizes the current day, not the next day's return.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TRIN</td>\n<td>(Advancing issues / declining issues) ÷ (advancing volume / declining volume)</td>\n<td>A low reading is associated with bullish conditions in the chapter; denominator components must be nonzero.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mutual-fund cash ratio</td>\n<td>Fund cash / total fund assets</td>\n<td>Cash can reflect expected redemptions or risk management as well as potential buying.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Simple moving average</td>\n<td>Sum of the specified closes / number of closes</td>\n<td>A backward-looking average depends on the chosen window.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OBV</td>\n<td>Add volume after a higher close and subtract after a lower close</td>\n<td>The starting level is arbitrary and the rule is not proof of predictability.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test an anomaly</td>\n<td>Compare a realizable strategy with a suitable risk benchmark after costs</td>\n<td>Consider sampling error, data selection, execution and changing conditions.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l9s-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Final integrated exercise</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>An investor says, \"Prices are efficient, so I should never study a company; and because a chart indicator worked last year, I can use it to beat the market.\" Evaluate both claims and create a decision process. A strong response explains that analysis contributes to price discovery and risk assessment; distinguishes a historical rule from reproducible after-cost, risk-adjusted evidence; and relates any strategy to diversification, the investor's horizon and the possibility of behavioral bias.</p>\n<p><em>Source note:</em> Based on <code>pdf/</code> (Pearson, 2014); scenarios and marking guidance are original. The source's short-interest explanation refers to future \"sales\" to cover: closing a conventional short requires buying shares. Historical technical indicators and anomaly examples are not verified current trading advice or guarantees.</p></div></details></div></details>");
